\f1\b\fs28 Request\f0\b0\fs24 \par
Hi team - before I sign off the Q4 cross-sell budget I need proof that targeting beats our random contacts. We now have the July-September outcomes for the 30 June customer snapshot. Can you run our cross-sell pipeline on it, tell me who takes up another product and why, and show me which 4,000 customers it would have picked and how many of them actually converted versus random? Opted-in customers only, and gender can't be used. Brief, data dictionary and the deck template are in the folder - need it for the 15 October planning meeting.\par
\par
\f1\b\fs28 Task Description\f0\b0\fs24 \par
Pre-existing assets: 30-Jun-2026 customer snapshot (input 1), data dictionary (input 2), campaign brief (input 3), summary deck template (input 4), and the team's existing cross-sell pipeline in 2_Code (pipeline_config.py and notebooks 01 preprocessing, 02 training, 03 testing, 04 back-test and packaging) built for an earlier snapshot and not yet run on this extract.\par
1. Review the campaign brief (Word) and data dictionary (Excel) to capture budget (4,000 contacts, $4 each, $150 margin), compliance rules (opt-in only, no gender, exclude 2+ complaints), acceptance criteria and leakage suspects.\par
2. Profile the raw extract with Excel PivotTables - conversion by product holding, region label inconsistencies, conversion by welcome_pack_sent (leakage), income missingness by segment - and save the pivot workbook (artifact 1).\par
3. Run the preprocessing notebook on the new extract and fix two schema-drift failures (mixed DD/MM and ISO dates; untrimmed region labels); review de-duplication, sentinel handling and missing-value treatment; outputs cleaned data and data-quality log (artifacts 2-3).\par
4. Run the training notebook and fix a baseline pipeline without an imputer and a newly added leaky column (welcome_pack_sent) that inflated cross-validated ROC-AUC to 0.98; compare logistic regression and gradient boosting on 5-fold CV (artifacts 4-5).\par
5. Run the testing notebook and fix an evaluation bug that scored class labels instead of probabilities; review hold-out ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, decile lift, calibration and SHAP drivers against the acceptance criteria (artifacts 6-7).\par
6. Run the back-test notebook: 5-fold out-of-fold scores, compliance filters, top 4,000 customers (artifact 8), and package the final model with metrics and README (final output 3).\par
7. Validate the back-test in Excel - count, compliance checks, expected vs actual conversions, profit vs random selection (final output 1) - and complete the summary deck from the template with results and the lift chart (final output 2).\par
Validation criteria: hold-out ROC-AUC >= 0.78; beats the logistic baseline on PR-AUC; top-20% lift >= 2.5x; no leaky or protected fields used; back-test list has exactly 4,000 compliant customers scored out of fold and converting at >= 2x the eligible average.\par
